  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nUpper level flow remains west to southwesterly over southern Texas.\nA surface low over the northern gulf will keep northwesterly winds \nthrough the rest of the work week, shifting from the east as we head \ninto the weekend. This flow will keep PWAT values between 0.7\"-1.0\" \nwhich is lower than the climate average for November, thus \ninhibiting chances of rain inland through the week. High \ntemperatures today in the mid 70s across the CWA and reaching into \nthe lower 60s tonight. Moving through the week temperature patterns \nwill remain constant with highs in the upper 70s and lows in the \nupper 50s to mid 60s. Minor coastal flooding conditions expected, \nassociated with northwesterly winds and longer wave periods (9 \nseconds). This will also keep the rip current risk moderate. \n\nThe subtropical jet will begin to appear this weekend advecting \npacific moisture into the upper levels. That, along with onshore \nflow will raise PWAT values to 1.35\"-1.45\" which is above climate \nnormals this time of year. With this increase of moisture and a \nfrontal passage likely Saturday into Sunday, rain chances are low to \nmoderate (20-30%). The GEFS is hinting at another stronger front \nmoving through the area Monday to Tuesday, while the ENS is wanting \nto move this system Tuesday into Wednesday. Timing is low confidence \nat this time, future runs will increase confidence as we move into \nnext week.",
